{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means keeping what serves you and reducing visual clutter—not making your flat empty.**\n\nFor a small flat, that usually means:\n- **A few well-sized pieces** rather than lots of small furniture, with clear walking routes.\n- **Useful storage**, especially closed cupboards that hide everyday clutter.\n- **A consistent palette:** two or three main colours, with wood or textured fabrics for warmth.\n- **Simple shapes and selective decoration:** leave some surfaces clear, but keep objects you love.\n- **Complete, comfortable spaces:** seating still needs a reachable table and good light; minimalism shouldn’t remove essentials.\n\nIn your combined living-and-dining room, that could mean distinct sitting and eating areas linked by similar colours and materials. The goal is **less visual noise, not less comfort**—and it doesn’t require replacing everything you own.\n\nI can discuss your flat, but cannot check or change its layout until its geometry is supported.", "conversationId": "676b7d6e77b442fbb1bc3735e45a26fd"}
